From: airoha-port <airoha@local>
Subject: [PATCH] net: phy: tolerate -EBUSY from request_module in phy_device_create

phy_request_driver_module() already tolerates -ENOENT (no modprobe in
initramfs).  request_module() returns -EBUSY when the usermode helper
cannot be spawned at all; that is equally harmless when the PHY driver
is built into the kernel, but it currently propagates out of
phy_device_create() and kills the whole device.

This breaks DT-described PHYs that cannot be probed by reading
registers, e.g. the AN7583 internal GPHYs which carry an explicit
ethernet-phy-idXXXX.YYYY compatible.

Signed-off-by: airoha-port <airoha@local>
---
--- a/drivers/net/phy/phy_device.c	2026-10-09 17:07:50.415506602 +0800
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/phy_device.c	2026-10-09 17:07:50.415506602 +0800
@@ -568,9 +568,16 @@
 	/* We only check for failures in executing the usermode binary,
 	 * not whether a PHY driver module exists for the PHY ID.
 	 * Accept -ENOENT because this may occur in case no initramfs exists,
-	 * then modprobe isn't available.
+	 * then modprobe isn't available.  Also accept -EBUSY: that is what
+	 * request_module() returns when the usermode helper cannot be run
+	 * (e.g. busy or not present).  PHY drivers that are built into the
+	 * kernel do not need a modprobe round-trip at all, and failing the
+	 * whole phy_device_create() over it breaks DT-described PHYs whose
+	 * only means of discovery is an explicit ethernet-phy-idXXXX.YYYY
+	 * compatible (the AN7583 internal GPHYs).
 	 */
-	if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_MODULES) && ret < 0 && ret != -ENOENT) {
+	if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_MODULES) && ret < 0 && ret != -ENOENT &&
+	    ret != -EBUSY) {
 		phydev_err(dev, "error %d loading PHY driver module for ID 0x%08lx\n",
 			   ret, (unsigned long)phy_id);
 		return ret;
